00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:07.830
And also this is quite a problematic part

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:10.020
I think of volume one.

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:13.120
And the traditional
interpretation of this passage

00:41:13.120 --> 00:41:17.023
is that for Marx, industrialization leads

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:23.590
to the concentration of a
production in big factories,

00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:28.177
the concentration of workers
in industrial cities.

00:41:28.177 --> 00:41:32.860
And so these really would translate

00:41:32.860 --> 00:41:36.760
in the growing class
consciousness and making basically

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:39.950
such a revolution in inevitable.

00:41:39.950 --> 00:41:43.463
But according to the kind
of dominant point of view,

00:41:43.463 --> 00:41:48.310
this kind of interpretation
of view of the dynamic

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:51.180
of working class power
would be obsolete given

00:41:51.180 --> 00:41:54.390
the shift of industrial
production towards East Asia

00:41:54.390 --> 00:41:57.510
and declining power of the
working class in the West.

00:41:57.510 --> 00:42:00.310
And so this would be one of the many,

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:05.310
Marx's predictions that proved
to be completely obsolete.

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:07.090
But from my point of view,

00:42:07.090 --> 00:42:12.033
Marx's his argument is a
bit more complex than this,

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:16.893
and I'm just moving to the next slide.

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:21.540
Well, the first thing to say
about these is that for Marx,

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:23.980
the movement for the
reduction of the working day

00:42:23.980 --> 00:42:28.130
is the premise for any
advancement of the working class.

00:42:28.130 --> 00:42:31.620
And the demand for shorter hours is key

00:42:31.620 --> 00:42:35.190
because it targets the
link between exploitation

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:37.420
and impoverishment that is at the center

00:42:37.420 --> 00:42:40.740
of the process of capital
accumulation and creates

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:42.900
the conditions to build solidarity

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:46.760
between employed workers and
be unemployed native born,

00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:50.100
and immigrant workers and
also between different genders

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:51.520
and the generations.

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:56.117
In particular working time
is key to link struggles

00:42:56.117 --> 00:42:59.340
around production and social reproduction.

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:01.819
And these are demand that the
labor movement put forward

00:43:01.819 --> 00:43:05.310
in different national
context and also became part

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:07.133
of an international struggle.

00:43:08.830 --> 00:43:13.690
For Marx support for the most
suppressed workers is crucial

00:43:13.690 --> 00:43:16.420
for the advancement of the working class.

00:43:16.420 --> 00:43:19.909
And in particular, he makes
the example of English,

00:43:19.909 --> 00:43:24.909
white male workers, who on
the one side behave like

00:43:25.957 --> 00:43:29.700
a slave dealers selling
their children and wife,

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:33.698
but also max notes that
it was that struggle

00:43:33.698 --> 00:43:36.580
that achieved the
shortening of working hours

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:38.230
for women and children,

00:43:38.230 --> 00:43:42.760
which also ended up
benefiting male adult workers.

00:43:42.760 --> 00:43:46.210
And the extension of factory
legislation put an end

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:49.140
to the most extreme forms of exploitation

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:50.820
in the domestic industry,

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:54.410
which were kind of 19th century
sweat shops that relate on

00:43:54.410 --> 00:43:56.730
the patriarchal family brutally

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.290
to exploited women and children.

00:43:59.290 --> 00:44:04.190
And so the extension of a
factory legislation enhance

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:06.350
the process of concentration of production

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:09.240
in big factories and industry centers,

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:12.750
which resulted from an
interplay of technological

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:14.173
and political factors.

00:44:15.508 --> 00:44:16.670
So from this point of view,

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:20.190
the class struggle is
not a secondary factor

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:22.090
in the development of capitalism,

00:44:22.090 --> 00:44:26.160
but actually shapes
capitalism development itself.

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:28.060
And for the same reason,

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:30.780
we could say that the
process of concentration

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:33.900
of industrial production in big factories

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:37.400
or industrial centers is not definitive

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:40.363
and Marx was actually quite aware of this

00:44:40.363 --> 00:44:43.560
and in the 1867 address
that he wrote on behalf

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:46.470
of the international, the general council

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:48.450
to its Roseanne Congress,

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:51.520
he argues that in order
to oppose the workers,

00:44:51.520 --> 00:44:53.180
the employers in England,

00:44:53.180 --> 00:44:55.620
either bring in workers from abroad

00:44:55.620 --> 00:44:58.330
or as transfer manufactured to countries

00:44:58.330 --> 00:45:01.113
where there is a cheap labor force, yeah.

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:06.370
So it's not like he
wasn't aware of processes

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:08.560
of production restructuring,

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:11.320
but by presupposing
capital's field of action

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:14.460
to be fully globalized
from my point of view,

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:18.730
capital makes it possible to
understand what Beverly silver

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:23.250
called the dialectic between
what resistance to expectation

00:45:23.250 --> 00:45:26.240
and capital efforts to
overcome that resistance

00:45:26.240 --> 00:45:28.910
by constantly revolutionizing production

00:45:28.910 --> 00:45:30.700
and social relations.

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:35.690
So these constant process
of spatial reorganization

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:38.730
that is a part of this dialectic

00:45:38.730 --> 00:45:41.620
is however based on labor cooperation

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:44.830
and so it could not
slippers all over the world

00:45:44.830 --> 00:45:49.830
and creates new sources of
structural power for workers.

00:45:50.120 --> 00:45:52.303
So at the same time,

00:45:52.303 --> 00:45:55.140
it's a weekends from some points of view,

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:58.683
but also empowers workers in new way.

00:45:59.760 --> 00:46:03.560
And an example of this are the struggles

00:46:03.560 --> 00:46:08.560
of immigrant workers in
the logistics sector,

00:46:10.920 --> 00:46:12.470
both in the United States.

00:46:12.470 --> 00:46:16.810
But I started in
particular Italy that even

00:46:16.810 --> 00:46:21.810
if they're often very precarious
dependent on the document

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:27.514
and so in a kind of
immune immigrant status,

00:46:27.514 --> 00:46:31.250
logistics workers have a huge potential

00:46:31.250 --> 00:46:35.470
to disrupt supply chains
through strikes and blockades.

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:38.230
And so for any talent redoing your newest,

00:46:38.230 --> 00:46:42.370
other Milani in a warehouse
where fresh food is stored,

00:46:42.370 --> 00:46:44.930
a four hour blockade means between

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:49.180
200 and 300,000 years last year.

00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:52.490
And these kinds of disruptive
potential helps explain

00:46:52.490 --> 00:46:54.670
why over the last 10 years,

00:46:54.670 --> 00:46:57.800
the movement of logistics
workers in Italy has been

00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:00.410
able to achieve substantial improvements

00:47:00.410 --> 00:47:03.020
in both wages and working conditions,

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:06.890
even though the labor
conditions are more generally

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:11.890
in the country have worsened
quite substantially.

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:17.810
And I think that this kind of
contradiction re-emerge is now

00:47:17.810 --> 00:47:22.110
in the wake of a global 19,
because on the one side,

00:47:22.110 --> 00:47:25.790
the pandemic is leading
to a massive job crisis

00:47:25.790 --> 00:47:28.970
with the mass unemployment increasing

00:47:28.970 --> 00:47:31.470
the competitive pressures on workers.

00:47:31.470 --> 00:47:34.490
And so in this way, the
pandemic is revealing

00:47:34.490 --> 00:47:39.390
